---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../lib/chart-spec'

const pay = assertion('the-biggest-employer-is-not-the-biggest-payer')
const commute = assertion('half-the-jobs-here-are-held-by-outsiders')
const moved = assertion('the-factories-left-lima-and-not-the-county')

const movedSpec = bar(
  moved.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  moved.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: "Lima's share, per cent", labelValues: true },
)

// County, city and the difference, at the seven censuses that print both rows on one page.
const factories = [
  ['1929', '6,588', '4,548', '2,040', '69.0'],
  ['1939', '6,164', '4,770', '1,394', '77.4'],
  ['1947', '14,709', '11,361', '3,348', '77.2'],
  ['1954', '14,691', '11,073', '3,618', '75.4'],
  ['1958', '13,526', '6,018', '7,508', '44.5'],
  ['1963', '14,897', '5,346', '9,551', '35.9'],
  ['1967', '16,400', '6,700', '9,700', '40.9'],
]

const paySpec = bar(
  pay.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  pay.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Average annual pay' },
)

const commuteSpec = bar(
  commute.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  commute.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'People', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Base
  title="Work"
  lede="What work there is in Allen County, what it pays, and how many of the people doing it live somewhere else."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Thirteen phases of counting jobs, and none of counting pay</h2>
    <p>
      This corpus has held the county's employment since its ninth phase — by sector, back to 1969,
      through the manufacturing decline and out the other side. It had never held a wage. The
      Quarterly Census of Employment and Wages publishes both, and the two rank the county
      differently.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={pay} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="pay"
      title="Average annual pay by sector, 2024"
      caption="Quarterly Census of Employment and Wages, annual averages, all ownerships. Pay is total annual wages divided by average annual employment — the file publishes both terms and not this ratio."
      columns={['Sector', 'Average annual pay']}
      rows={pay.figures.map((f) => [f.label, '$' + f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="pay" set:html={JSON.stringify(paySpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Across all 49,690 covered jobs the average weekly wage is $1,131 — 89.3 per cent of Ohio's
      and 77.8 per cent of the country's. A worker here earns about four fifths of what a worker in
      the United States does.
    </p>
    <p>
      Manufacturing is 17.9 per cent of the jobs and 28.0 per cent of the wages, the only sector in
      the county whose share of pay is more than half again its share of employment. The period this
      site calls deindustrialization ended with manufacturing still the best-paid large sector here;
      what replaced the jobs did not replace the pay. The corpus holds one year of wages and cannot
      say when that gap opened.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Half the jobs here belong to somebody else's county</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={commute} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="commute"
      title="The journey to work, 2022"
      caption="LEHD Origin-Destination Employment Statistics, all jobs. In-commuters come mostly from the ring of rural counties — Auglaize, Putnam, Van Wert, Hardin, Hancock."
      columns={['', 'People']}
      rows={commute.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="commute" set:html={JSON.stringify(commuteSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Lima holds 46.1 per cent of the county's jobs and 37.2 per cent of its employed residents, so
      the city is a net importer of workers inside a county that is a net importer of workers. And
      the three flows are alike in what they pay: 47.1, 50.8 and 49.4 per cent of them are in the
      file's top earnings band. The obvious story — that the good jobs go to people who drive in —
      is not in the data.
    </p>
    <p>
      One figure on this page is reported and not read. Franklin County — Columbus, a hundred miles
      away — is the second-largest recorded destination for Allen County residents who work outside
      it, above two counties that share a border. The 1,723 are spread thinly across many Columbus
      tracts. A block-to-block table of where people work cannot be published as collected, and this
      is what a disclosure-protected file looks like at small grain: the sums are sound and the
      cells are not. The county totals are trusted here because an unrelated programme counted
      49,690 jobs where this one counted 48,730.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The factories left Lima and they did not leave the county</h2>
    <p>
      Between the 1929 census of manufactures and the federal employment series that opens in 1969
      lay forty years this corpus could not see. It expected the county's industrial peak to be
      somewhere inside them — the Depression at one end, the tank plant and the war in the middle.
      Five volumes on a shelf nobody had walked hold six censuses of those years, and the peak is
      not in them. The highest figure any of them returns is 16,400 in 1967, and the modern series
      opens two years later at 17,623 and tops out at 18,400 in 1973.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={moved} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="moved"
      title="Lima's share of Allen County's factory workforce, 1929–1967"
      caption="Census of Manufactures, Ohio chapters. The first two censuses count wage earners and the last five count all employees, which is why the share is drawn and the levels are not: the city and the county change column together on the same page, so their ratio survives a break the two levels do not."
      columns={['Census', 'County', 'Lima', 'Outside Lima', "Lima's share"]}
      rows={factories}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="moved" set:html={JSON.stringify(movedSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Four years did it. In 1954 Lima held 11,073 of the county's 14,691 factory jobs; in 1958 it
      held 6,018 of 13,526. The city lost 5,055 people and the county lost 1,165, so the ground
      outside the city line went from 3,618 to 7,508 — it more than doubled. Across the nine years
      to 1963 the two move almost exactly against each other: Lima down 5,727, the outside up 5,933,
      the county up 206.
    </p>
    <p>
      The city did not lose factories. It had 84 establishments in 1954 and 88 in 1958, and the
      average one went from 132 people to 68. All three of the county's large industrial
      installations stand outside every municipality — the refinery and the tank plant in Shawnee
      Township, the Ford engine plant in Bath — and the newest of them was under construction on
      Bible Road in September 1957, which this corpus dates from a mechanic's lien rather than from
      anything a census says. The census names no establishment, so what it shows is the shape and
      not the cause.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two other things fell out of the volumes. The Depression cost this county 6.4 per cent of its
      factory workforce against Ohio's 16.0, and cost Lima nothing at all — the city came out of the
      decade with 222 more wage earners than it went in with. And a third of the county's 1967
      workforce built vehicles: 5,400 people in nine establishments, taking $23.0 million of the
      $32.8 million spent on new plant in the county that year.
    </p>
  </section>
  <script>
    import { renderCharts } from '../scripts/charts'
    renderCharts()
  </script>
</Base>
